{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover every obligation in `requirements.references.markdown_literals`, and their assertions enforce the expected outcomes.\n\n**Escaped roles stay literal.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal]` runs `preprocess_book` on `\\{impl}…`. It asserts that all 3 marker occurrences survive and that no `habeas-req-ref-impl` badge appears.\n- The same test checks that an escaped backslash does not escape the role: `\\\\{marker}` still counts as a reference.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal]` and `src/terms.rs#mod[tests]/fn[term_dependencies_of_every_markdown_extension_skip_literal_roles]` repeat the escaped case. They run it over `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD` and `.Markdown`, with `.txt` as the non-Markdown control. `tests/definitions.rs#fn[literal_term_roles_in_subject_and_requirements_inputs_need_no_definition]` covers it for `{term}`.\n\n**Inline code and fenced code stay literal.**\n- Both are exercised in the tests above.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` adds backtick and tilde fences, indented code, and fenced directives nested in code or HTML.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown]` shows that a role inside a fenced block in a requirement body is not counted.\n\n**Raw HTML stays literal, and text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers an HTML block, a block comment, an inline comment, and an inline tag attribute (`<span title=\"…\">`). It asserts that only the role between `<em>…</em>` is a reference, which gives 1 reference and 1 badge. It also checks that plain text gets no HTML protection (5 roles).\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` does the same for fenced directives. It covers a block, a comment and a tag attribute, and keeps the one fence outside HTML.\n\n**A `{req}` directive in a fenced code example does not define a requirement, and a legacy inline `{req}` role does not either.**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal]` covers a `:::{req} block` inside a code fence, the legacy `{req}`legacy``, and inline-code `{req}`. A real directive in the same text still renders as a definition.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal]` repeats the legacy-role case. Further `definition_blocks` tests cover related cases.\n- `definition_blocks` is the one parser shared by book rendering, evidence loading (`collect_definitions`) and term scanning. Testing it through `replace_definitions` therefore covers every definition path.\n\n**Markdown versus non-Markdown.** The multi-extension tests use `.txt` as a control, so the Markdown-only scope of the literal rule is asserted.\n\nI found no plausible violating behavior that these tests would accept, and no gaps. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments.",
